  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Human Trafficking in Medieval Europe is already showing early scholarly uptake and clear reader interest. OpenAlex records 2 citations, and usage data from OAPEN shows 3,766 downloads, indicating that the book has been actively consulted since publication.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Christopher Paolella\u2019s Human Trafficking in Medieval Europe is an open-access scholarly book that traces the deep history of trafficking from Late Antiquity to the Early Modern Period. Its impact profile is still early, but it is not quiet: the book has been cited twice in the scholarly record and has drawn substantial download activity, suggesting both visibility and use within its field.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record points to an emerging scholarly conversation around classical and ancient history. The two citing works appeared in 2024 and include a book and a book chapter, both in adjacent historical research areas. That pattern suggests the book is beginning to circulate as a source for researchers working on broader questions of antiquity, historiography, and historical narrative.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage data provide the strongest signal at this stage. The book\u2019s OAPEN metrics record 3,766 downloads, which is consistent with active readership for an accessible academic monograph. Because the work is available in open-access form, these downloads are a meaningful indicator of reach beyond the citation record, including readers who may be using the book for research, teaching preparation, or reference.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Other signals are more limited or not yet captured in the available record. There is no event archive data to summarize, and no public-discourse metrics are available here. The book is held in WorldCat, which supports discoverability in library collections, but the digest does not provide course-listing evidence or other direct teaching-adoption claims. Overall, the record shows a book with early scholarly traction and strong reader access, with influence likely still developing.</p>",
